# Expense Tracker Project Rules

You are working on a portfolio project called:

Expense Tracker | ACME

Follow these rules for all changes in this project.

## Stack

Use:

- React
- Vite
- JavaScript
- HTML5
- CSS3
- Recharts
- Lucide React
- localStorage

Do not introduce:

- TypeScript
- Redux
- Tailwind CSS
- Bootstrap
- Firebase
- backend services
- databases
- unnecessary dependencies

Do not install a new dependency unless it is clearly necessary.

## Development Style

Keep the project beginner-friendly.

Prefer:

- simple React components
- clear props
- React state
- custom hooks where useful
- reusable utility functions
- meaningful variable and function names
- semantic HTML
- accessible controls

Avoid:

- unnecessary abstraction
- over-engineering
- giant components
- duplicated logic
- deeply nested component structures
- complicated patterns when a simpler solution works

## Project Workflow

The project is being built phase by phase.

Do not implement functionality from future phases unless explicitly requested.

When the user asks to continue:

1. Work only on the current phase.
2. Do not rewrite unrelated working code.
3. Preserve existing functionality.
4. Test the changes.
5. Explain what was changed.
6. List created and modified files.
7. Explain how to test the phase.
8. Suggest one concise Conventional Commit message.
9. Stop and wait for the user before moving to the next phase.

Do not automatically start the next phase.

## Git

Use concise Conventional Commit messages such as:

- chore: initialize expense tracker project
- feat: add application layout
- feat: add expense data model
- feat: add expense form
- fix: validate expense form
- style: improve responsive dashboard
- docs: update README

Do not commit automatically.

The user uses GitHub Desktop.

## Architecture

Keep the structure understandable and maintainable.

Current preferred structure:

src/
  components/
  data/
  hooks/
  utils/

Use subfolders for components when it improves organization.

Do not create files only for the sake of creating files.

## Components

Prefer small reusable components.

Components should have one clear responsibility.

Do not put all application logic inside App.jsx.

Keep App.jsx focused on top-level application composition and state coordination.

## State Management

Do not use Redux.

Use React state and custom hooks.

As expense functionality grows, prefer a custom hook such as:

useExpenses()

for:

- expenses
- adding expenses
- updating expenses
- deleting expenses
- persistence
- related expense state logic

Keep filtering and calculations separated when appropriate.

## Expense Data

Use a consistent expense object model.

Preferred shape:

{
  id: "unique-id",
  title: "Groceries",
  amount: 45.90,
  category: "Food",
  date: "2026-08-23",
  description: "Weekly groceries"
}

IDs must be unique.

Do not rely on array indexes as IDs.

## Categories

Keep categories centralized in one reusable source.

Initial categories:

- Food
- Transport
- Shopping
- Bills
- Entertainment
- Health
- Travel
- Other

Do not duplicate category arrays across components.

## Currency

Default currency is EUR.

Use a reusable formatter for currency.

Do not manually concatenate currency symbols throughout components.

## Dates

Store dates in a consistent machine-readable format.

Use reusable date formatting utilities for display.

Avoid duplicated date formatting logic.

## localStorage

Use reusable storage utilities.

Handle:

- missing data
- invalid JSON
- malformed stored values

The application must not crash because localStorage contains invalid data.

## Design

The application should look like a clean modern SaaS dashboard.

Design goals:

- professional
- minimal
- spacious
- consistent
- responsive
- portfolio-quality

Use:

- CSS variables
- consistent spacing
- subtle borders
- rounded cards
- clear visual hierarchy
- subtle hover and transition effects

Do not scatter hardcoded colors throughout components.

## Responsive Design

Support:

- mobile from 320px
- tablet from 768px
- desktop from 1024px
- large desktop around 1440px

Avoid horizontal scrolling.

Forms, cards, navigation and charts must remain usable on mobile.

## Accessibility

Use:

- semantic HTML
- proper labels
- real buttons for clickable actions
- keyboard navigation
- visible focus states
- accessible form errors
- aria-label only where needed

Do not use clickable div elements when a button is appropriate.

## Validation

Do not rely only on browser validation.

Show clear validation messages.

Expense form requirements:

Title:

- required
- minimum 2 characters

Amount:

- required
- greater than 0
- reasonable maximum

Category:

- required

Date:

- required

Description:

- optional

## Charts

Use Recharts only.

Charts must:

- use real expense data
- be responsive
- have readable tooltips
- handle empty states
- work on mobile

Do not hardcode fake chart statistics into production logic.

## Error Handling

Handle errors gracefully.

The UI should not crash because of:

- invalid localStorage
- empty data
- invalid expense values
- missing chart data

## Performance

Keep the app lightweight.

Avoid:

- unnecessary re-renders
- excessive dependencies
- premature optimization

Prioritize readable code first.

## Final Quality

Before considering a phase complete:

- check for console errors
- check unused imports
- check responsive behavior where relevant
- preserve existing working behavior
- avoid introducing functionality planned for later phases
